<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John de Britto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1647–1693</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as João de Brito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John de Britto was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> Mission of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Adopting the dress and way of life of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ascetic, he made many converts in the Marava country of Ramnad. After one convert, a local chief, dismissed his additional wives, the Sethupathi ruler Kizhavan had him beheaded at Oriyur in 1693. He was canonised in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-de-britto.jpg" alt="John de Britto" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cleetuscls · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._John_De_Britto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
